🐛 Describe the bug
# pyright: strict
import torch

@torch.no_grad()
def double(x: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:
    return 2 * x

reveal_type(double)
makes pyright report error: Untyped function decorator obscures type of function; ignoring decorator
This is because _NoParamDecoratorContextManager.__new__ is untyped:



      pytorch/torch/utils/_contextlib.py


        Lines 158 to 164
      in
      a4a0378







           class _NoParamDecoratorContextManager(_DecoratorContextManager): 




               """Allow a context manager to be used as a decorator without parentheses.""" 









               def __new__(cls, orig_func=None): 




                   if orig_func is None: 




                       return super().__new__(cls) 




                   return cls()(orig_func) 





Versions
torch==2.10.0a0+git34d6ef7 compiled from source
cc @lolpack @maggiemoss @ndmitchell @kinto0 @samwgoldman

pytorch/torch/csrc/utils/device_lazy_init.cpp


        Lines 79 to 92
      in
      a60d9e1







           void register_fork_handler_for_device_init(at::DeviceType device_type) { 




           #ifndef WIN32 




             auto& flag = at_fork_once_flags[static_cast<int>(device_type)]; 




             c10::call_once(flag, [device_type]() { 




               static at::DeviceType at_fork_device_type = device_type; 




               pthread_atfork(nullptr, nullptr, []() { 




                 set_device_in_bad_fork(at_fork_device_type, true); 




                 if (is_device_lazy_init_supported(at_fork_device_type)) { 




                   set_requires_device_init(at_fork_device_type, true); 




                 } 




               }); 




             }); 




           #endif 




           } 





Thanks to @guangyey for refactoring the poison fork function to be device-agnostic.
Regarding register_fork_handler_for_device_init, there are some points worth discussing.
Based on the implementation of this function, it appears to be designed to support multiple devices simultaneously. However, it only supports the first device registered through this function; subsequent registrations will not work properly. The root cause lies in the following code: static at::DeviceType at_fork_device_type = device_type;. at_fork_device_type is initialized on the first call.
However, we can draw a line from torch.accelerator: only one device can exist within a Python program, so the current register_fork_handler_for_device_init should still work.
So, the question is: should we refactor it to truly support multiple devices simultaneously, or refactor it to support only one device to avoid ambiguity?
